WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:14.290
A lot of art from the Middle Ages is so bizarre,
it seems… beyond explanation.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:19.590
Which is why it’s no surprise that these
seemingly nonsensical images have become popular

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.849
sources of memes in recent years.

00:00:21.849 --> 00:00:27.849
It’s hard not to look at these images and
ask yourself… wait, what is going on here?

00:00:27.849 --> 00:00:32.400
So, for this entry into the archive, I try
to track down the real meaning behind some

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.930
of these images that have circulated on the
internet without context.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.600
But keep in mind that what I’ve found in
my research is that for many illustrations…

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.260
medieval art historians are just as clueless
as the rest of us.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.359
With that said, let’s begin.

00:00:46.359 --> 00:00:51.270
To start, we have the curious trend of medieval
babies who look… a little off.

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:56.060
The sheer amount of… unfortunate looking
babies in medieval (and later Renaissance)

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.800
art suggests more is going on than just a
few misplaced brush strokes.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.920
Portraits of medieval infants seem like they
were intentionally styled to look like tiny,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.250
middle aged men.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.520
And indeed, that is more or less what’s
going on here…

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.250
These artists are following the trend of the
‘homunculus,’ which translates to “little

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.619
man” in Latin.

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:19.979
The idea behind the homunculus is related
to the baby Jesus — who was the most popular

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:21.890
infant to paint at the time.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:27.190
Due to a very literal interpretation of the
church’s idea that Jesus was born ‘fully

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:32.190
formed and unchanged,’ a lot of classic
religious art from the era has a very adult-faced

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.190
Jesus.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:38.110
And paintings of other infants started to
follow this trend as well: hence, babies being

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.680
painted with receding hairlines and layers
of muscle.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:42.680
Pretty interesting stuff.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.200
By the time Renaissance styles developed over
more of Europe, however, the somewhat unsettling

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.229
trend of adult-faced infants went out of practice
— and frescos thankfully started featuring

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:54.960
much more normal looking babies.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.239
Switching topics, another unusual theme in
medieval art that the internet has picked

00:01:59.239 --> 00:02:03.740
up on is that in scenes of people meeting
their untimely demise — which should be

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.410
pretty grim, the sufferers seem strangely
laid-back.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:12.401
Seriously, almost all these people seem…
pretty chill about their imminent death, all

00:02:12.401 --> 00:02:13.530
things considered.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:18.910
In fact, a lot of them appear somewhat bored,
or at most subtly annoyed by the situation,

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.569
which is an unusual reaction to such a serious
event.

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:27.030
So why are there entire battle scenes where
everyone seems completely checked out?

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.530
Well, one explanation is that the neutral
expressions come from the cultural value of

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.879
greeting death with calmness so you’ll be
rewarded in what comes after.

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:40.870
While staying calm and grounded can be healthy,
I will say, I think some of these people might

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.310
be taking their chillness a little too far.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.930
And while we’re on the subject of strange
portrayals of death — death itself, as personified

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:53.730
by skeletal figures coming to haunt the living
— often looked… a little goofier than

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.650
one might expect, which has led to a variety
of jokes online.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:02.810
Most of these illustrations are actually related
to the concept of the Danse Macabre, or Dance

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:03.810
of Death.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:08.359
The Danse Macabre was a concept that death
was an equalizer, coming for all classes of

00:03:08.359 --> 00:03:13.620
feudal society; the images were designed to
remind people to see this life’s fleeting

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.489
nature and to embrace death when the time
came.

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:22.540
A related — and also strangely amusing — image
that has become popular online is this portrait

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.939
of three individuals having a bizarre reaction
to a group of skeletons.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:32.459
The image is actually based on a legend called
‘The Three Living and the Three Dead,’

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:37.280
in which three nobles encounter three skeletons,
who are actually undead reflections of the

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.330
nobles — sent to frighten them into changing
their immoral ways.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:45.940
It’s a pretty unique tale with a lot of
variations, and one which shows up a fair

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.680
amount in medieval art.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.420
One particularly strange trend in medieval
art that has captured online attention is

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:56.159
how some artists depicted…… rabbits.

00:03:56.159 --> 00:04:01.730
Various manuscripts portray rabbits not as
we know them — as small, fuzzy animals that

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:06.189
aren’t much of a treat to a human — but
as large, threatening creatures carrying off

00:04:06.189 --> 00:04:07.189
helpless villagers.

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:11.760
It’s a concept that certainly stands out
in the margins of illuminated texts.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.069
So, what’s going on here?

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:16.310
Are these some kind of mythical beasts?

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.549
Did rabbits have some kind of attitude problem
in the middle ages?

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:24.160
Well, the most accepted theory is that these
strange, hilarious images are meant to be

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.790
just that — jokes.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:30.140
Just like how we share funny images today,
this sort of bizarre art was probably something

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:42.170
medieval monks had a laugh about with their
friends.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:47.090
Briefly leaving the medieval era for a series
of portraits I had to include, we have the

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:52.300
highly unusual 18th century self-portraits
of French painter Joseph Ducreux.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.960
Although trained in the classical style, the
successful Ducreux became unsatisfied with

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.720
the constraints of traditional portraiture,
which at the time were almost entirely serious,

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.620
with somber body language and reserved compositions.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:09.700
So, Ducreux started creating art with absurd,
larger-than-life expressions that really go

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:11.350
against the standards of the time.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:17.160
In breaking these constraints, his works resembled
the earlier 16th and 17th century style of

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.890
portraiture called tronies: which were paintings
like the one you see before you that frequently

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.810
featured exaggerated facial expressions.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.220
Some of these expressions are so unusual that
it might seem hard to believe they aren’t

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.320
modern parodies.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.500
But despite what most classic portraits might
lead you to believe, some people from the

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.450
past had a very modern sense of humor — and
Ducreux was clearly one of those people.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:44.390
Today, his most famous image is probably this
one, where he painted himself pointing at

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:46.610
viewers and laughing at them.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:50.360
It says something that this image is still
funny to people on the internet hundreds of

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.540
years after it was painted.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:56.371
Before we reach our final topic, there are
a few other miscellaneous images I’d like

00:05:56.371 --> 00:05:57.440
to try and address.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.270
First, we have this amusing picture of what
seems to be a mythical bird monster carrying

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:07.170
a smaller creature and a human away on its
back — and the two passengers appear so

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:11.450
deep in argument they haven’t noticed they’re
being hoisted away.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:15.450
Despite appearances, this isn’t a medieval
illustration, but a depiction of a costumed

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:19.990
individual at a 17th century fair — with
the figures on their back likely some kind

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:21.190
of puppets.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:22.190
Pretty unusual.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:26.430
Next, we have this image of a man who seems
like he’s being wrapped up in a sheet by

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:27.800
his friends.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.550
Despite the goofy vibe, the image is actually
likely Vitalis of Milan, a Saint, who, according

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.620
to legend, was sentenced to death.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.860
So those two aren’t his friends, but his
executioners, in all likelihood lowering him

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:40.020
into his grave.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:44.700
And finally, on a lighter note, we’ve got
this famously weird portrait of a baby.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:49.900
And no, this isn’t one of the bizarre medieval
era portraits, but a stylized portrait by

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:55.830
20th and 21st century figurative artist Fer-nan-do
Botero — whose unique style sometimes causes

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:59.030
people to mistake his work from something
from centuries prior.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:04.850
And to close out this entry, we have a topic
I’ve touched on in the past, but is always

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.250
worth revisiting.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:10.970
That being the bizarre medieval trend of depicting
knights fighting snails.

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:15.260
You can find these bizarre showdowns hidden
within the margins of many manuscripts.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:20.160
There’s a strangeness here that is pretty
clear — I mean, snails don’t seem like

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.270
the most obvious creatures to depict as gargantuan,
menacing juggernauts.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:29.040
And maybe I’m overanalyzing, but to me there’s
a sense in these images that the knights…

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.290
might not make it.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:32.480
These snails seem like they mean business.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:37.600
And this wasn’t a one-off concept — we
have over sixty-five documented images of

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.010
snails doing battle with various warriors.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:45.000
And what’s most intriguing is we’re not
totally sure why — with theories ranging

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:51.430
from the snails representing class divisions,
to being a joke about the concept of a knight’s

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:55.050
armor, to the slow and steady approach of
death.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:59.950
Or, maybe this is another example of medieval
artists trying to create something to make

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:02.600
their friends laugh — just like what we
do online today.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:06.600
…And that’s all the medieval art we have
time for in this entry.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:11.909
I’ve made other videos covering weird medieval
depictions of animals and mythical creatures

00:08:11.909 --> 00:08:15.710
— that explain additional bizarre illustrations
I didn’t get to here.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:16.950
Thanks for watching.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:22.050
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.990
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:25.840
See you in the next video.

